+
+Go 1.22 library and CLI. `Export` copies newline-separated records from a
+borrowed reader to its owned writer, returns the accepted record count, and
+closes the writer exactly once on every path. Each record is written before
+reading the next. Finalization errors are returned to the caller.
+
+`ExportLimit(r, w, limit)` exports at most `limit` records. Zero means
+unlimited; a positive value stops after that many records. A negative value
+returns `ErrInvalidLimit` before reading or writing, while still closing the
+owned writer. `Export` remains unlimited and source compatible. A lone operation
+or close error is returned directly. If both fail, both causes are inspectable
+with `errors.Is` or `errors.As`. A final unterminated record counts, as does
+each empty record.
+
+CLI usage is `lineexport INPUT OUTPUT [LIMIT]`. Omitting the limit or passing
+zero exports every record. A positive limit exports a prefix; a negative limit
+fails. Success exits 0 without stdout; operational failures exit 1 with stderr
+and no success message. Invalid argument count or a non-integer limit exits 2.
+Accepted output may already exist on failure; publication is not atomic. Paths
+are positional, including names beginning with `-`. No external dependency is
+needed.
